#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065326} :activity/id #uuid "35485642-6f34-523f-ba9d-b8883802af55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168 :topic/id #uuid "e02cd9a7-60ac-54e8-865a-ffe6fe24b944" :topic/math-academy-id 369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064128 :question/id #uuid "ceddd310-5fe4-5b55-8210-991ff22c66b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"} :step/id #uuid "9ce63c6b-93ff-55b3-823f-2b9e85238652" :step/math-academy-id 27273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065387 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nradians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z$. Relative to the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nthe sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) &= \\frac{y}{z} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{y}{z} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dz}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h}\n$$\n\nso plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "da3b2021-8bcc-506c-8874-9ce60aca7594" :tutorial/math-academy-id 501 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3981e94-4f89-5b37-84f2-1d3e0cbb41c1" :step/math-academy-id 934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :question/id #uuid "a669adb9-c12e-520d-994b-cddf4f9fedb6" :question/math-academy-id "e-233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064905}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"} :step/id #uuid "72b9ba59-522f-50d2-bed7-143439adb60f" :step/math-academy-id 935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065388 :tutorial/content "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nas shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure, $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z$. Relative to the angle $\\theta$, the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x}{z} \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos θ) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\\n- \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n- \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{4\\sin θ} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\csc θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9abef4d4-d050-57eb-8e0a-61634d35af20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 503 :tutorial/title "Calculating Rates of Varying Angles Using Trigonometry"} :step/id #uuid "70026486-4a5b-5b48-bfe7-c78c5c312ef0" :step/math-academy-id 939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064030 :question/id #uuid "7c740ffe-3391-52cc-b8fd-7fb9a54e58cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064904}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"} :step/id #uuid "804402e9-5068-546f-a006-16edfa5e5a69" :step/math-academy-id 940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065177}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064455}]}
